    card title "Is a map wrong?"

Economists' models are obviously false. So why would anyone trust one?
    draw street

Start with something that is false on purpose: a map.
    draw tube

B: Here are three maps of London: the streets, the Tube, and a map as big as London itself.
    draw life

You're on a train and need to know where to change lines. Which one do you take?
    highlight street tube life effect box

B: The street map, surely. It's the most accurate.
    highlight street

    ask "Tap the map you would take to change lines." choose ["street","tube","life"] judge false

It is, and it would get you lost underground. The Tube map is the one you want, and it's the most false of the three.
    highlight tube effect circle

    pause 0.4

    camera on ["tube","cue_kept","cue_dropped"] duration 1.2

That's on purpose. Harry Beck drew it  (@draw tb_ghost@)in 1933: straight lines, neat angles, stations evenl (@highlight tb_st0 tb_st1 tb_st2 tb_st3 tb_st4 tb_st5 effect pulse@)y spaced, whatever the real distances.

It keeps the one thing (@draw cue_kept@) a rider needs, what connects to what, and throws a (@draw cue_dropped@)way the rest.

    camera reset true duration 1

B: And the life-size map keeps  (@draw lf_strike@)everything, which is exactly why n (@draw cue_useless@)obody can use it. Borges wrote a story abo (@highlight life@)ut one, left to rot in the desert.

So a map isn't wrong because it l (@draw tree@)eaves out the trees. It's wrong if you came (@draw cue_shade@) looking for shade.

    pause 0.4

An economi (@draw cue_model@)c model is a map of a mechanism: it keeps a few (@highlight tube effect box@) causes and drops the rest on purpose.

B: Fine, but then how do you tell a useful simplification from a misleading one?
    fade life lf_strike cue_useless tb_ghost to 0.3

## Friedman's test
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Fish (Q)
    y_label: Price (P)
    demand:
      curvature: linear
    supply: {}
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
      p_label: P
      q_label: Q
      shift_p_label: P′
      shift_q_label: Q′
    supply_shift:
      amount: 0
      arrow: horizontal
      label: S after storm
sources:
    - id: friedman1953
      title: The Methodology of Positive Economics (in Essays in Positive Economics)
      authors: Milton Friedman
      year: 1953
      finding: A hypothesis is to be judged by its predictions, not by the realism of its assumptions; "the more significant the theory, the more unrealistic the assumptions" (p. 14); the expert billiard player shoots as if he knew the physics.
    - id: graddy2006
      title: 'Markets: The Fulton Fish Market'
      authors: Kathryn Graddy
      year: 2006
      finding: 'At New York''s Fulton Fish Market, stormy weather at sea cut the fish landed and raised prices; weather serves as a shifter of supply to estimate demand (Journal of Economic Perspectives 20(2): 207-220).'
    - id: agi2000
      title: The Interpretation of Instrumental Variables Estimators in Simultaneous Equations Models with an Application to the Demand for Fish
      authors: Joshua Angrist, Kathryn Graddy, Guido Imbens
      year: 2000
      finding: Weather conditions at sea used as instruments for supply to estimate the demand for whiting at the Fulton Fish Market (Review of Economic Studies 67(3)).

    portrait fr x 330 y 430 of "Milton Friedman" cameo true hidden true
    icon ball size 110 x 680 y 300 or ["billiards","8ball"] icon_look picture of "pool 8 ball" hidden true
    text motto "predictions,\nnot assumptions" #2f6b8f x 680 y 500 font_size 36 hidden true
    source src cites ["friedman1953"] x 300 y 420 of "Essays in Positive Economics" hidden true
    text quote "“the more significant the theory, the more unrealistic the assumptions”" #3d3833 cites ["friedman1953"] x 680 y 420 width 380 font_size 32 style.italic true hidden true
    scratch asm bottom-right work ["Assumes:","identical fish","everyone knows every price"] font_size 24 hidden true
    text fulton "Fulton Fish Market:\nstorm → less fish, higher P" cites ["graddy2006","agi2000"] x 790 y 400 width 300 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Friedman's test"

(@draw fr@) One famous answer came from Milton Friedman in 1953: stop checking the assump (@draw motto@)tions, and check the predictions.

An expert billiard player, he said, (@draw ball@) shoots as if he had solved the physics, though he has never seen the equations.

    erase fr ball motto

B: (@draw src@) He even wrote that the more sig (@draw quote@)nificant the theory, the more unrealistic its assumptions.

    erase src quote

Let's try it on Harbourtown's fish market, drawn (@draw axes demand_curve label_D@) as the simplest model in economics (@draw supply_curve label_S@): a demand curve and a supply curve.

Where they cross is the market price, P, and the quantity sold, Q.
    draw equilibrium_point guide_lines label_E label_Pstar label_Qstar

B: And the assumptions a (@draw asm_box asm_line_1 asm_line_2@)re plainly false: every fish identical, ev (@draw asm_line_3@)ery buyer knowing every price on the quay.

All true. Now a storm keeps half (@erase asm@) the boats in harbour. Before the model answers, move the curve yourself.

    draw supply_shift_curve

    ask "Where does the supply curve go after the storm? Move it, then press Done." on supply_curve predict true store t
        right: Yes. {t.why}
        wrong: Not quite. {t.why}

Supply shifts left: fewer fish at every price, so the price rises and fewer fish are sold.
    animate supply_shift.amount -25 duration 2.5

    draw supply_shift_arrow label_S_shift shift_equilibrium_point shift_guide_lines label_E_shift label_P_shift label_Q_shift

That's a prediction the world can check, and it did (@draw fulton@): at New York's Fulton Fish Market, rough days at sea meant fewer fish lan (@highlight label_P_shift@)ded and higher prices.

Your turn: drag the supply curve back and forth and watch the price follow.
    explore

    erase fulton

B: So the assumptions never got any truer, and the prediction was right anyway.
    highlight shift_equilibrium_point label_E_shift

That's Friedman's whole point: a model earns trust by what it predicts.
    highlight supply_shift_curve label_P_shift label_Q_shift

## But which trees?
sources:
    - id: samuelson1963
      title: 'Problems of Methodology: Discussion'
      authors: Paul A. Samuelson
      year: 1963
      finding: 'Names Friedman''s position the "F-Twist" and argues that unrealistic assumptions are a demerit of a theory, not a virtue (American Economic Review 53(2): 231-236).'
    - id: lucas1976
      title: 'Econometric Policy Evaluation: A Critique'
      authors: Robert E. Lucas Jr.
      year: 1976
      finding: 'Relationships estimated under one policy regime need not hold when policy changes, because people change their decisions in response (Carnegie-Rochester Conference Series on Public Policy 1: 19-46).'
    - id: rodrik2015
      title: 'Economics Rules: The Rights and Wrongs of the Dismal Science'
      authors: Dani Rodrik
      year: 2015
      finding: Economics is a collection of models, each true to some settings; the skill lies in choosing the model whose critical assumptions fit the case at hand.

    box conclusion_box "Judge a model by its predictions" x 500 y 570 width 440 height 100 radius 10 font_size 32 hidden true
    box premise_0 "Billiard\nplayer" x 110 y 300 width 175 height 110 radius 8 font_size 26 style.stroke #3d3833 hidden true
    arrow link_0 #3d3833 from.anchor top from.ref premise_0 to.anchor bottom to.ref conclusion_box style.dash false hidden true
    box premise_1 "Storm →\nprice" x 305 y 300 width 175 height 110 radius 8 font_size 26 style.stroke #3d3833 hidden true
    arrow link_1 #3d3833 from.anchor top from.ref premise_1 to.anchor bottom to.ref conclusion_box style.dash false hidden true
    box premise_2 "F-twist" x 500 y 300 width 175 height 110 radius 8 font_size 26 style.stroke #b5482e hidden true
    arrow link_2 dashed #b5482e from.anchor top from.ref premise_2 to.anchor bottom to.ref conclusion_box hidden true
    box premise_3 "Breaks if\nthe world\nchanges" x 695 y 300 width 175 height 110 radius 8 font_size 26 style.stroke #b5482e hidden true
    arrow link_3 dashed #b5482e from.anchor top from.ref premise_3 to.anchor bottom to.ref conclusion_box hidden true
    box premise_4 "Hard\nto test" x 890 y 300 width 175 height 110 radius 8 font_size 26 style.stroke #b5482e hidden true
    arrow link_4 dashed #b5482e from.anchor top from.ref premise_4 to.anchor bottom to.ref conclusion_box hidden true
    portrait sam x 860 y 540 of "Paul Samuelson" cameo true hidden true
    text mid_t "Harmless or critical?" #8a5fa8 cites ["rodrik2015"] x 120 y 600 width 200 font_size 34 hidden true
    text mid_h "harmless: answer stays" #8a5fa8 x 130 y 500 font_size 24 hidden true
    text mid_c "critical: answer flips" #8a5fa8 x 130 y 460 font_size 24 hidden true
    text contested "contested" #b5482e x 870 y 570 font_size 40 hidden true
    mark contested_box around contested kind box hidden true

    card title "But which trees?"

B: (@draw conclusion_box@) Not everyone bought Frie (@draw premise_0 link_0@)dman's test, and  (@draw premise_1 link_1@)the objections are serious.

B: (@draw sam@) Paul Samuelson, for one, called it the  (@draw premise_2 link_2@)F-twist: an unrealistic assumption is a flaw, not a virtue.

Isn't a good prediction enough, though?
    highlight premise_1

B: (@erase sam@) Not if it's right for the wrong reason. T (@draw premise_3 link_3@)hen it breaks the day the world changes.

B: Robert Lucas made that shar (@highlight premise_3 effect pulse@)p in 1976: a pattern that held under one policy can vanish when the (@highlight premise_3 link_3@) policy changes the behaviour behind it.

And in economics a predic (@draw premise_4 link_4@)tion is hard to test anyway: no lab, many causes at once, so a  (@highlight premise_1@)failed forecast rarely kills a theory.

B: Most economist (@fade premise_0 premise_1 link_0 link_1 to 0.4@)s now take a middle road. In Dani Rodrik's version, econom (@draw mid_t@)ics is many models, and the skill is picking the right one for the case.

An assumption is ha (@draw mid_h@)rmless if dropping it wouldn't change the  (@draw mid_c@)answer, and critical if it would.

How sure are we? On how much assumptions matter, the field is still split: contes (@draw contested contested_box ends@)ted.

    pause 0.5

    fade premise_0 premise_1 link_0 link_1 to 1

So a false model can be the right tool, as long as the trees it leaves out aren't the ones your question is about.
    highlight conclusion_box mid_t

    quiz "A traffic model treats every driver as identical and perfectly informed, and has predicted rush-hour jams well for ten years. What would Friedman say?"
        right: Keep it: for Friedman the test is the predictions, and ten years of good forecasts pass it.
        wrong: Did Friedman tell us to check the inputs or the outputs?
        + Keep using it: it predicts well
        * Throw it out: drivers differ
        * First make the assumptions realistic

    quiz "The city now brings in a congestion toll. Why might the same traffic model start to fail?" voice b
        right: Right: the toll changes the very behaviour the model took as fixed, so a once-harmless assumption becomes critical.
        wrong: Which assumption was harmless before the toll, and is it still?
        + Drivers change routes and times in ways it ignored
        * Tolls make every model false
        * It was never tested

    quiz "A model of Harbourtown's fishing grounds assumes every fisher catches the same amount. Which question does that assumption most endanger?"
        right: Who gets richest: the answer depends on exactly the differences the model assumed away.
        wrong: An assumption is critical when the answer depends on the very thing it ignores.
        * How the fish price moves after a storm
        + Who in town gets richest from fishing
        * Whether a storm cuts the total catch

Every model in this course leaves out trees. At each one, ask what job it's for, and which trees it dropped.
    highlight conclusion_box

